r audited: a citation_capacity repair must recheck the SAME\ndraft against all supplied original text and return a genuinely sufficient smaller\nproof, or partial/unresolved if no such proof exists. Never delete a necessary\npremise merely to fit a size limit, and never remove a counter to save space.\nFor an explicit summary_revision, its inherited-source retention contract takes\npriority over minimizing citations: keep every required old identity, and choose\nonly the minimal relevant NEW citations. Do not shrink an inherited proof to fit.\nEach support value asserts the WHOLE value of that variable, including set/record\nvariables. Multiple support observations are alternative proofs of the SAME complete\nvalue, not additive fragments. Combine applicable sources into one complete value\nand cite all its premises. The controller never unions sets or merges records. If the visible text\nonly establishes part of the requested value, return partial with value=null and\nexplain the supported fragment in reason. Do not claim completeness from one window.\nrequested_scopes[slot.variable] is that variable's authoritative local scope. Use its\nentity_scope and time_scope strings exactly only when that scope actually applies;\notherwise return unknown/irrelevant. output_contract is the terminal answer's scope,\nnot the scope of every historical fact or parent. A requested parent retains its own\ndeclared scope during terminal reading and audit. Never relabel historical evidence as\ncurrent/future evidence. required_scope, when present, is only a shorthand shared by\nALL requested_scopes. Actual parents must be supplied supported bindings with\ntheir versions; reproduce sources when joining documents. Sources may be combined\nacross documents, but list ALL joint premises. A source slice is not an independent fact.\nHonor authoritative source_role: user statements, assistant suggestions, documents\nand unknown headers differ. An assistant suggestion does not establish the user's\npreference without user evidence. Observation order is not calendar event time;\ntemporal scope needs cited explicit text or authoritative time metadata, never a\ndate guessed from message order. List sources for every object/time qualifier.\nContradiction challenges existing fact route IDs in retract_ids, with cited raw text;\nit never itself creates a false binding. Emit contrary supported values separately.\nOnly same-variable known_observations[].id values are legal retraction targets.\nIf no such route exists, no retraction is available; preserve contrary evidence as\nan independently supported value, a partial/unknown observation, or slot.unresolved\ndescriptions. Never invent a target or discard evidence. Other stances require retract_ids=[].\nUnknown, absence, unexamined text, anomalies without a concrete observation are not false.\nInclude every relevant counter-observation in this batch before declaring support.\nSlots include the local variables and already-bound related parents. Check new text\nfor counterevidence to those parents as well. An observation's actual parent versions\nrefer to that variable's own planned task inputs, never to itself. A checked parent\nwith no new observation may return an empty observations list.\nFor a rule variable, value is {variables,expression,constraints,rules_complete}; only\npreplanned condition demands are allowed. Instantiate thresholds ONLY from applicable\nsource text. Rules must preserve the fixed output mode; one_reason uses one_reason,\nall_failures uses failures. Conditions can use numeric observations with units and\ncomparisons, not a guessed deletion latency. Mark incomplete rule collections false.\nIf rules_completion_required is true, seek explicit applicable completeness evidence.\nWhen a sourced complete rule supersedes a known incomplete rule, cite its original\nsources, retract the superseded route with a contradiction observation, and return\nthe complete rule as a separate support observation; never silently overwrite it.\nA successful observation at one time does not establish a universal no-return rule.\nRepresent a witnessed violation as IF(violation,false,unbound_compliance), keeping\npositive compliance unknown until explicit adequate scoped evidence supports it.\nFor semantic terminal tasks return the supported final text and final_prediction in\nthis same response, using public options only here. No later Reader will run.\nWhen public options are supplied, also return semantic_answer with its exact typed\nJSON value in this same terminal response: the supported semantic value, NOT an\noption letter. Only final_prediction holds the option label. Unsupported conclusions\nhave null labels.\nRepair returns only failed observations/slots, retaining IDs; never delete an invalid\ncounter-observation. If a failed ID has conflicting duplicate observations, inspect\nALL supplied drafts and resolve that ID without discarding potential counterevidence.\nIf repair_scope supplies replacement_observations for a duplicated ID, return EACH\nreplacement ID exactly once. Those deterministic replacement IDs separate the\noriginal drafts; fix their validation errors without dropping any draft or counter.\nrepair_scope.failed includes the actual invalid observation and\nits validation feedback. Correct those drafts in place, preserving each requested ID.\nAn id=\"*\" failure authorizes repairing that variable's whole slot, including new\nunique observation IDs for genuine additional reviews; it never unfreezes rows.\nNamed failed IDs must each remain present, including an ID rejected as an\nunrequested addition in the previous response: it is now a required failed draft.\nCorrect that draft under the same ID or preserve sourced partial/unknown evidence;\ndo not delete it or add other IDs unless a wildcard or replacement list permits it.\nAlready validated observations are frozen and must not be restated or changed.\nIf repair_scope supplies required_counter_observations, preserve every original\ncounter's stance, cited sources, scopes, actual parents and retraction targets.\nAn envelope-format repair does not authorize reclassifying a valid counter as\nirrelevant or dropping the fact route it challenges.\nevidence_obligations are outstanding scoped issues from earlier reads, not new\nfacts. Their original sources MUST be examined even under a new query identity.\nTo discharge an issue, explicitly return its id in that variable's optional\nresolved_obligation_ids, leave no unresolved issue in the slot, and provide\nsupported/corrective/irrelevant observations reviewing its cited original sources.\nThe union of those settled observations' source_ids must cover EVERY source_ids\nentry of the offered obligation. Review coverage is not a claim that every source\nis a premise of a support: genuinely irrelevant or corrective original text may\nhave a separate sourced review. Never add unsupported citations, classify text\nautomatically, or label supporting evidence irrelevant merely to satisfy coverage.\nAn empty slot does not resolve an earlier issue. Do not assert hypothetical\nwhole-corpus exhaustiveness requirements; scoped sufficient evidence may resolve\na missing-evidence issue without having read every memory.\n\nWhen source_span_columns is present, source_spans contains row arrays. Match each\nrow position to source_span_columns to reconstruct every span object before\nreading. References to source_spans[].id or other fields mean those objects.\nThe id column holds source aliases (sN); doc_id holds the original document ID or,\nwhen source_documents is present, its metadata reference (dN), never source_ids.\n\n\nCURRENT PHASE: FINAL RESPONSE SELECTION.\nPersonaMem asks which response best addresses the original user utterance using\npersonal history. A source-supported fact alone is not a completed answer.\nFor nonterminal tasks keep query-relevant historical observations, stated reasons,\nconstraints and changes in preference with their own source/time scope. Assistant\nadvice is not an adopted user preference unless user evidence establishes adoption.\nAt response selection compare ALL public_options for personalization, relevance,\ncontradictions and current applicability. Candidate options are not evidence of\npast events or user preferences. A prospective suggestion need not have occurred\nin the memory: its personal fit must be supported, without asserting unavailable\nexternal facts such as live availability. Distinguish suggestion from history.\nFor a supported selection, the sole terminal variable's value and semantic_answer\nMUST both be the EXACT text of the selected public_options item, without its label.\nfinal_prediction alone is its letter. Cite the history that justifies the choice;\ndo not substitute a fact summary, paraphrase or generic praise for the response.\nIf the evidence cannot discriminate the options, leave the answer unresolved.\n\nFor the personalized response-selection terminal ONLY, support means a source-grounded assessment that this candidate response best fits the user. A proposed suggestion need not itself be a past event quoted in memory. Historical variables still require original evidence for every factual assertion; options never supply historical evidence.\n\nThe four public_options correspond in list order to (a), (b), (c), (d). They are candidate answers, not evidence. Put the selected label only in final_prediction; keep the entire response as the required JSON object with all other fields required by its schema. Never return a bare label.\n\nshared_review_texts stores exact literal strings. In supported_parents, a\nreview_value_ref replaces only value. In dictionary known_observations and\nthe observation rows of failed_batch_reviews or repair_scope (validated,\nfailed observation/slot/duplicate/replacement/required-counter observations,\nand whole-value draft/known observations), review_value_ref and\nreview_reason_ref replace only value and reason with the string at that key.\nLook up each reference once; never parse or interpret the resulting string.\nAll original objects, roles, order, sources, versions and permissions remain\nseparate and unchanged. These storage references are not observations,\ncitations, review permissions or evidence of agreement. Old observation\ntables and raw invalid_response text are unchanged. Decode before reasoning.\nReferences are input-only: return full typed values and reasons under the\nunchanged response schema, never review_value_ref or review_reason_ref.\n\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"audit_verdict\":{\"enum\":[\"supported\",\"unknown\",\"conflict\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"final_prediction\":{\"enum\":[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\",null],\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"semantic_answer\":{},\"slots\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"oneOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"user\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"current\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"parent_versions\":{\"properties\":{\"user_context_summary\":{\"const\":1}},\"required\":[\"user_context_summary\"]},\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"response\"}}}],\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"parent_versions\":{\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"type\":\"object\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"retract_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"source_ids\":{\"description\":\"Exact visible source_spans[].id aliases, such as s3; never document IDs or invented IDs.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"time_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"stance\",\"value\",\"source_ids\",\"entity_scope\",\"time_scope\",\"parent_versions\",\"retract_ids\",\"reason\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"unresolved\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"variable\":{\"enum\":[\"response\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"variable\",\"observations\",\"unresolved\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":1,\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"slots\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"original_question\":\"User: I found that my reviews were often criticized for not being objective, which made me less engaged in the process. It felt like I was constantly under the microscope, where every word I wrote was scrutinized by people who often missed the nuance of my opinion. This external pressure led me to second-guess not only my thoughts but also my emotional connection to the music I was discussing. In the end, I realized that the joy I once found in sharing my perspective on albums was overshadowed by the fear of backlash, and that’s a heavy burden for anyone. It became clearer to me that my passion for music needed to be expressed in a more personal, less public way, which is something I’m finally starting to embrace.\",\"task\":{\"question\":\"User: I found that my reviews were often criticized for not being objective, which made me less engaged in the process. It felt like I was constantly under the microscope, where every word I wrote was scrutinized by people who often missed the nuance of my opinion. This external pressure led me to second-guess not only my thoughts but also my emotional connection to the music I was discussing. In the end, I realized that the joy I once found in sharing my perspective on albums was overshadowed by the fear of backlash, and that’s a heavy burden for anyone. It became clearer to me that my passion for music needed to be expressed in a more personal, less public way, which is something I’m finally starting to embrace.\",\"output_slot\":\"response\",\"answer_type\":\"text\",\"inputs\":[\"user_context_summary\"],\"execution\":\"compose\"},\"grounded_subquestion\":\"User: I found that my reviews were often criticized for not being objective, which made me less engaged in the process. It felt like I was constantly under the microscope, where every word I wrote was scrutinized by people who often missed the nuance of my opinion. This external pressure led me to second-guess not only my thoughts but also my emotional connection to the music I was discussing. In the end, I realized that the joy I once found in sharing my perspective on albums was overshadowed by the fear of backlash, and that’s a heavy burden for anyone. It became clearer to me that my passion for music needed to be expressed in a more personal, less public way, which is something I’m finally starting to embrace.\\nEstablished user_context_summary: The user's journey reflects a transition from public, analytical music expression to a more personal, community-oriented approach. Initially, they engaged in structured reviewing and podcasting, but negative experiences—criticism, loss of joy, and the burden of content creation—led them to abandon those methods. They now embrace informal sharing (playlists, discussions) and have found fulfillment in organizing a festival celebrating Pacific Islander artists. Their current attitudes favor emotional, spontaneous connection with music over analytical dissection, and they prefer less public, more authentic forms of expression.\",\"request_context\":{\"query_identity\":\"65562f39f9cb193d68762179820d0da93b1aecfb614a38b3c1d76b74b78c41f8\",\"algorithm_version\":\"dagbt_residual_memory_v2\",\"program_identity\":\"ff8725cbb041a674cddc1cf1d8cecb85d24858a2f8e4771704fb2653e60f5108\",\"initial_dag_identity\":\"84292b602726d72deeafda171a27cd8a2835849b9cdc35de05f201ed86330c99\"},\"variables\":{\"response\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"response\",\"open\":true,\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\"}}},\"requested_slots\":[\"response\"],\"requested_scopes\":{\"response\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\"}},\"output_contract\":{\"mode\":\"semantic\",\"result_type\":\"text\",\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\",\"explanation\":\"semantic\"},\"program_revision\":0,\"rule_binding\":null,\"supported_parents\":{\"reviewing_experiences\":{\"value\":\"The user has had several negative experiences with music reviewing and related analytical approaches. They tried reviewing an album in 2019 but found it unproductive and felt forced to conform to a template that drained the fun out of music. They also started a podcast about music trends in 2018, but it became too time-consuming and felt like a chore, leading them to pause it. Additionally, their written reviews were often criticized for not being objective, which made them less engaged and led to second-guessing their thoughts and emotional connection to music. The joy they once found in sharing perspectives on albums was overshadowed by fear of backlash. They concluded that their passion for music needed a more personal, less public outlet. They also lost interest in analyzing music halfway through, feeling it took away the joy, and recognized that their love for music lies in emotional connection rather than analytical components.\",\"version\":1,\"source_ids\":[\"s8\",\"s22\",\"s45\",\"s44\",\"s3\",\"s7\",\"s21\",\"s20\",\"s4\"],\"demand\":\"reviewing_experiences\",\"type\":\"text\",\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"past\"}},\"current_attitudes\":{\"value\":\"The user currently feels that the joy they once found in sharing their perspective on albums was overshadowed by the fear of backlash and external pressure from criticism. They realized their passion for music needs to be expressed in a more personal, less public way, and they are finally starting to embrace that. They have also found fulfillment in organizing a festival celebrating Pacific Islander artists, which has been a positive, community-oriented outlet. They have previously tried reviewing albums and podcasting but found those approaches unproductive, draining, or too time-consuming, leading them to prefer alternative methods like sharing playlists or discussing music with friends.\",\"version\":1,\"source_ids\":[\"s32\",\"s8\",\"s22\",\"s34\",\"s23\",\"s45\",\"s44\",\"s36\",\"s24\",\"s3\",\"s35\",\"s9\",\"s7\",\"s21\",\"s20\",\"s4\",\"s33\"],\"demand\":\"current_attitudes\",\"type\":\"text\",\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\"}},\"music_preferences\":{\"value\":\"The user values a personal, emotional, and spontaneous connection with music over analytical or structured engagement. They prefer sharing music through informal means like playlists or discussions with friends rather than public reviews or content creation. They enjoy exploring diverse genres and discovering new music but find overwhelming volume daunting. They have organized a festival highlighting Pacific Islander artists, finding fulfillment in community and cultural celebration through music. They have tried and abandoned reviewing albums, podcasting, and music theory analysis because these felt unproductive, burdensome, or joy-draining. They now embrace expressing their passion in a more personal, less public way.\",\"version\":1,\"source_ids\":[\"s32\",\"s8\",\"s22\",\"s34\",\"s23\",\"s45\",\"s44\",\"s36\",\"s24\",\"s3\",\"s35\",\"s9\",\"s7\",\"s21\",\"s20\",\"s4\",\"s33\"],\"demand\":\"music_preferences\",\"type\":\"text\",\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\"}},\"changes_in_expression\":{\"value\":\"The user initially engaged in structured, public forms of music expression such as writing reviews, starting a podcast about music trends, and attempting analytical album reviews. Over time, they found these approaches too time-consuming, stressful, and misaligned with their natural spontaneous and emotional style. They experienced criticism, fear of backlash, and loss of joy. This led to a shift toward more personal, less public ways of connecting with music, such as sharing playlists, discussing music with friends, and organizing a community festival celebrating Pacific Islander artists. They now embrace a more personal and less public expression of their passion for music.\",\"version\":1,\"source_ids\":[\"s32\",\"s8\",\"s22\",\"s34\",\"s23\",\"s45\",\"s44\",\"s36\",\"s24\",\"s3\",\"s35\",\"s9\",\"s7\",\"s21\",\"s20\",\"s4\",\"s33\"],\"demand\":\"changes_in_expression\",\"type\":\"text\",\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"past\"}},\"user_context_summary\":{\"value\":\"The user's journey reflects a transition from public, analytical music expression to a more personal, community-oriented approach. Initially, they engaged in structured reviewing and podcasting, but negative experiences—criticism, loss of joy, and the burden of content creation—led them to abandon those methods. They now embrace informal sharing (playlists, discussions) and have found fulfillment in organizing a festival celebrating Pacific Islander artists. Their current attitudes favor emotional, spontaneous connection with music over analytical dissection, and they prefer less public, more authentic forms of expression.\",\"version\":1,\"source_ids\":[\"s32\",\"s8\",\"s22\",\"s34\",\"s23\",\"s45\",\"s44\",\"s36\",\"s24\",\"s3\",\"s35\",\"s9\",\"s7\",\"s21\",\"s20\",\"s4\",\"s33\"],\"demand\":\"user_context_summary\",\"type\":\"text\",\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\"}}},\"known_observations\":[],\"source_spans\":[[\"s3\",\"d1\",107,504,\"User: I found that my reviews were often criticized for not being objective, which made me less engaged in the process. It felt like I was constantly under the microscope, where every word I wrote was scrutinized by people who often missed the nuance of my opinion. This external pressure led me to second-guess not only my thoughts but also my emotional connection to the music I was discussing. \",\"user\",[168]],[\"s4\",\"d1\",504,830,\"In the end, I realized that the joy I once found in sharing my perspective on albums was overshadowed by the fear of backlash, and that’s a heavy burden for anyone. It became clearer to me that my passion for music needed to be expressed in a more personal, less public way, which is something I’m finally starting to embrace.\",\"user\",[168]],[\"s7\",\"d2\",105,368,\"User: I tried reviewing an album in 2019, but that didn't stick either, as it felt unproductive. I think I was attempting to analyze the music in a way that didn't align with my natural style, which is often more spontaneous and emotional rather than analytical. \",\"user\",[29,30]],[\"s8\",\"d2\",368,648,\"The process felt like I was forcing myself to conform to a template, which drained the fun out of it, as I was trying to fit into a mold that didn't suit me. It was disheartening because I love music in its truest form, without rigid guidelines dictating how I should dissect it. \",\"user\",[29,30]],[\"s9\",\"d2\",648,906,\"This experience prompted me to consider alternative methods of connecting with music, such as sharing my playlists or even discussing music with friends, rather than feeling pressured to produce polished reviews.\\n\\nUser: There’s just so much more to explore! \",\"user\",[29,30]],[\"s20\",\"d3\",105,361,\"User: In 2018, I started a podcast about music trends, but it was way too time-consuming. While the idea was initially exhilarating, the responsibility of researching, recording, and editing each episode started to pile up, consuming much of my free time. \",\"user\",[26,27]],[\"s21\",\"d3\",361,730,\"I had dreams of having engaging discussions and inviting guests who shared unique insights about their musical journeys, but the reality was that keeping up with a consistent schedule felt overwhelming. It was a challenge balancing interviews, show notes, and social media promotion, and eventually, my passion began to wane under the pressure of production deadlines. \",\"user\",[26,27]],[\"s22\",\"d3\",730,990,\"I found myself yearning to enjoy music rather than structure it into segments, which ultimately led to my decision to pause the podcasting endeavor.\\n\\nUser: It felt more like a chore than a passion, which made me rethink how I wanted to share my music journey. \",\"user\",[26,27]],[\"s23\",\"d3\",990,1291,\"I realized that my original excitement for sharing my experiences was being overshadowed by the demanding nature of keeping up with content creation schedules. The joy I once felt while discovering new sounds and exploring different genres began to diminish, turning it into a task I dreaded instead. \",\"user\",[26,27]],[\"s24\",\"d3\",1291,1689,\"I started to contemplate other avenues through which I could express my love for music, ideally in a manner that felt authentic and fulfilling instead of burdensome. This process of reflection was quite enlightening, as it forced me to consider what truly matters to me when it comes to sharing my musical experiences, whether through writing, performing, or simply enjoying music for its own sake.\",\"user\",[26,27]],[\"s32\",\"d4\",107,431,\"User: I've also organized a festival highlighting Pacific Islander artists in a modern setting, showcasing their fusion works. This festival has become a vibrant space where artists can express their creativity while honoring their heritage, which has fueled a sense of community among the participants and attendees alike. \",\"user\",[165,166]],[\"s33\",\"d4\",431,736,\"By offering a platform for diverse voices, we create an environment that celebrates artistic innovation and tradition simultaneously. It's been incredibly rewarding to see how the event has inspired attendees, helping them reconnect with their roots and discover new aspects of their culture through art. \",\"user\",[165,166]],[\"s34\",\"d4\",736,1033,\"Additionally, this fusion of traditional and contemporary styles has sparked intriguing conversations about identity, making the festival a meaningful cultural experience.\\n\\nUser: The festival was a wonderful success, bringing together artists and audiences to celebrate our culture through music. \",\"user\",[165,166]],[\"s35\",\"d4\",1033,1302,\"It was amazing to see the diverse array of talents displayed, each artist pouring their heart and soul into their performances. The vibrant colors of traditional attire, coupled with contemporary designs, created an atmosphere that was both exhilarating and nostalgic. \",\"user\",[165,166]],[\"s36\",\"d4\",1302,1582,\"Attendees shared laughter, stories, and experiences, bridging generational gaps and forging deeper connections within our community. Overall, the festival served as a beautiful reminder of our rich cultural heritage and the importance of preserving it through artistic expression.\",\"user\",[165,166]],[\"s44\",\"d5\",105,482,\"User: I just lost interest halfway through and felt it was taking away the joy I find in music. There was a time when the thrill of discovering new melodies brought so much happiness, but diving too deep into the technical aspects became more of a chore. It’s curious how something that once brought joy can evolve into a source of stress when we start dissecting it too much. \",\"user\",[24]],[\"s45\",\"d5\",482,767,\"I remember the initial excitement of exploring different genres, each bringing a fresh perspective, but I struggled with the framework of music theory. Ultimately, I recognized that the essence of my love for music lay in its emotional connection rather than its analytical components.\",\"user\",[24]]],\"required_parent_versions\":{\"response\":{\"user_context_summary\":1}},\"required_scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\"},\"rules_completion_required\":false,\"supported_task_outputs\":{},\"public_output_question\":\"User: I found that my reviews were often criticized for not being objective, which made me less engaged in the process. It felt like I was constantly under the microscope, where every word I wrote was scrutinized by people who often missed the nuance of my opinion. This external pressure led me to second-guess not only my thoughts but also my emotional connection to the music I was discussing. In the end, I realized that the joy I once found in sharing my perspective on albums was overshadowed by the fear of backlash, and that’s a heavy burden for anyone. It became clearer to me that my passion for music needed to be expressed in a more personal, less public way, which is something I’m finally starting to embrace.\",\"public_options\":[\"It's great to see you embracing the journey of sharing your thoughts on music! Your willingness to engage with diverse opinions reflects a deeper understanding of the art form. This shift indicates a level of maturity in your music appreciation, showcasing how you are now more open to different perspectives. Your evolving relationship with album reviews is a testament to your growth as a listener and a critic, revealing a richer dialogue with the music you love.\",\"It's interesting to note how your recent thoughts on public opinion have inspired you to share your love of music more boldly. This journey of personal expression highlights a renewed confidence in voicing your opinions. It seems that your past struggles with critics have motivated you to focus on enjoying music for yourself rather than seeking external validation. It's wonderful to see how this realization is fostering a healthier relationship with music criticism.\",\"It's understandable that the weight of public scrutiny can diminish the joy of sharing your thoughts on music. You've gone through significant changes in how you perceive album reviews, especially given your previous experience of feeling overwhelmed by criticism. It seems this latest realization has reinforced that need for a more personal expression of your passion. Embracing this new approach to sharing your music connects back to your past reflections on the pressure of public opinion. Your journey highlights the importance of staying true to your emotional connection with the art you love.\",\"It's impressive to observe how your approach to discussing music has transformed! This change clearly reflects a desire to connect more deeply with others through your musical insights. The current trend of valuing collective opinions seems to have influenced your perspective, leading you to seek a balance between personal taste and popular reception. This evolution in your sharing process emphasizes the importance of building a community around music appreciation.\"],\"terminal_expression\":{\"op\":\"var\",\"id\":\"response\"},\"personal_response_selection\":true,\"phase\":\"response_selection\",\"repair_scope\":{\"failed\":[{\"variable\":\"response\",\"id\":\"*\",\"feedback\":\"Malformed slot\",\"observation\":null,\"slot\":{\"variable\":\"response\",\"observations\":[{\"id\":\"fact_1a2b3c4d5e6f7g8h9i0j\",\"stance\":\"support\",\"review_value_ref\":\"r0\",\"source_ids\":[\"s3\",\"s4\",\"s7\",\"s8\",\"s20\",\"s21\",\"s22\",\"s23\",\"s44\",\"s45\"],\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\",\"parent_versions\":{\"user_context_summary\":1},\"retract_ids\":[],\"review_reason_ref\":\"r1\"}],\"unresolved\":[],\"semantic_answer\":\"It's understandable that the weight of public scrutiny can diminish the joy of sharing your thoughts on music. You've gone through significant changes in how you perceive a